/******************************************************************************
* djinterp [re_std]                                                       chrono
*
* <chrono> umbrella header:
*   Aggregates the granular headers under /inc/djinterp/re_std/chrono/.
* Mirrors the standard <chrono> convenience header and adds no symbols of
* its own.
*
*   WHAT THIS MODULE COVERS -- THE DURATION AND CLOCK CORE:
*
*     duration<Rep, Period>          the tick count + compile-time period
*     duration_values                the rep's zero / lowest / highest
*     treat_as_floating_point        the implicit-conversion opt-out
*     duration_cast                  explicit, truncating toward zero
*     floor / ceil / round / abs     the C++17 rounding set, back-ported
*     nanoseconds .. hours           C++11 typedefs
*     days / weeks / months / years  C++20 typedefs, back-ported
*     time_point<Clock, Duration>    a clock-tagged offset from an epoch
*     time_point_cast
*     system_clock                   wall clock, Unix epoch, not steady
*     steady_clock                   monotonic, for intervals
*     high_resolution_clock          a typedef for one of the above
*     is_clock                       C++20 trait, back-ported
*     the literals                   1s, 250ms, 2h, ...
*
*   WHAT THIS MODULE DOES NOT COVER -- THE C++20 CALENDAR AND TIME ZONES:
*   year, month, day, weekday, year_month_day and the rest of the civil
* calendar; the operator/ date-composition syntax; hh_mm_ss; the
* utc_clock / tai_clock / gps_clock / file_clock family and clock_cast;
* time_zone, zoned_time and the tzdb; and the <format> integration. None
* of it is present, and none of it is approximated.
*
*   That is the same call made for <bitset>, whose string constructor and
* stream operators are absent rather than half-built: a partial calendar
* is worse than no calendar, because year_month_day that is right about
* leap years and wrong about the Gregorian reform is a bug that reaches
* production. The time-zone half additionally needs a tzdata source,
* which is a deployment dependency rather than a coding problem. Both are
* tracked as a separate roadmap entry.
*
*   The consequence to know: days, months and years here are FIXED-LENGTH
* durations. `t + years(1)` advances by 31556952 seconds, the mean
* Gregorian year -- it does not give the same date next year. See
* duration_typedefs.hpp.
*
*   WHERE re_std IS AHEAD OF std IN THIS MODULE:
*     - floor / ceil / round / abs      C++17 -> C++11
*     - days / weeks / months / years   C++20 -> C++11
*     - is_clock                        C++20 -> C++11
*     - sys_time / sys_seconds / sys_days  C++20 -> C++11
*     - the duration and time_point mutators are constexpr from C++14,
*       where std waited for C++17
*
*   std::chrono is itself C++11, so the module's floor is C++11 and there
* is no back-port win on the core types. What re_std adds there is one
* implementation with one behaviour -- including a documented, honest
* answer for steady_clock::is_steady on platforms without a monotonic
* source, where implementations otherwise differ.
*
*
* path:      /inc/djinterp/re_std/chrono/chrono
* link(s):   TBA
* author(s): TBA                                           created: 2026.08.25
******************************************************************************/

